                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



dresses, on lawn chairs, on crates, on the gravel itself, a transistor radio
going from somebody’s lap, a pitcher of something cold passing hand
to hand. Nobody was talking much. The city was too quiet. When a
siren went by, far off, you could hear all of them turn their heads.
   In the corner, a boy of about nine sat with a pot lid in his lap.
   He hit it, once, with a spoon. A flat, ugly clang.
   “Nene, no,” said a woman, and a man beside her said, “Déjalo.”
Leave him. The boy hit it again. He wasn’t playing anything yet. He
was finding something out. A third hit. A fourth, with space in it.
   And I watched thirty adults who were afraid of the dark go still.
   I don’t know what the child was doing. I only know that when he
found the rhythm, or it found him, a man in the second row set down
his pitcher. A grandmother put a hand flat on her knee. Somebody’s
husband started tapping the arm of a lawn chair. Then somebody else,
a thigh. Then a girl had a bottle and a key. In ninety seconds the roof
was a drum, and the dark was only the dark, and a city that had lost its
lights had found, for one night, a way to hear itself.
   “Déjenlo tocar,” somebody said, softly, to the rest. Let him play.
   They listened to the kid.
   I’d like to say I’m not a person who tears up at a pot lid. I was
twenty-five years old on a gravel roof in 2026 with a hand on a drum
case and wet eyes, hearing the oldest rule in my family: listen to the little
ones.
   “You went,” said Manny.
   “Nineteen seventy-seven. A kid on this roof with a pot lid. They let
him play.”
   “I’d have let him play.” Manny took his hand off the case. He looked
at the sky for a second, at the city that had its lights on. “Probably he
was good.”
   “He was nine.”
   “Nine’s when I was good.”




